export const PRIVACY_POLICY_MD = "# Sidekick privacy policy\n\nLast updated: 27 November 2026\n\nSidekick is an AI assistant that runs entirely on your phone. This policy explains what it does with your information. In short: **we don't collect anything.** There are no accounts, no analytics, no ads and no tracking, and we have no servers that receive your data.\n\n## What stays on your phone\n\nEverything Sidekick sees, hears and remembers stays on your phone:\n\n- What you ask, and the answers, including pictures you ask Sidekick to draw, objects you erase from photos and photos you enlarge (all made on the phone).\n- Photos, video and audio from the camera and microphone, and the transcripts and notes made from them.\n- People, places, to-dos, reminders and facts it remembers.\n- Contacts, calendar events and notifications it reads, if you allow it.\n- Photos, folders and files you allow it to search (Settings → Phone search, off until you turn it on): on iPhone your whole photo library or only the photos you choose, on Android only the photo folders you pick (such as Camera and Screenshots), and only the folders and files you pick. Sidekick reads them on the phone and keeps only an encrypted index (the date, the place, the words in a photo and a description of it as numbers); your photos and files stay where they are and are never changed or sent anywhere. Turning it off, or removing a folder, deletes what was read from it.\n- Where you have been, if you turn on location: places are kept as points on the phone and named using a list of towns built into the app.\n- Phone activity you allow it to read, such as step counts, which apps you used and for how long (screen time), and text you copy, if you ask about it.\n- Your texts (SMS) and call history, only if Sidekick is your phone's digital assistant and you allow it: read on the phone when you ask (or when a routine you made asks), never copied into Sidekick's memory and never sent anywhere. Texts you approve are sent from your phone like any text, through your mobile network.\n- Files you share into Sidekick or attach in the chat (documents, spreadsheets, e-books, zips, pictures, recordings, videos), and files in folders you pick: read on the phone, the temporary copy deleted once read. What you ask about them is kept like any question, unless Off the record. A spreadsheet you change is saved only where you choose.\n- Routines you set up (for example \"every evening, recap my day\"): these run on the phone at the time you chose.\n\nAll of this is processed on the phone by AI models that run on the phone. The memory database is encrypted, with its key kept in the phone's secure key store. Location, if you allow it, is turned into a town name using a list built into the app, with no online lookup. Sidekick turns off Android's automatic cloud backup, so this data is not copied to Google's backup either.\n\nYou can see and delete any of it in the app (Memory, Storage and Privacy settings). You can export everything to a file. Uninstalling Sidekick deletes all of it.\n\n## When Sidekick uses the internet\n\nSidekick works offline. It connects to the internet only in these cases:\n\n1. **Downloading its AI models.** On first run, and later when a better model for your phone is available (by default only on Wi-Fi while charging; you can switch this off), Sidekick downloads model files from Hugging Face (huggingface.co) and GitHub (github.com, raw.githubusercontent.com). These are plain file downloads: nothing about you is sent. Like any website, those services can see your phone's IP address.\n2. **Online lookup — off unless you turn it on.** When it is on and a general question needs the web, a few search words for it (written on the phone, with the names of people you know taken out) are sent to Wikipedia and DuckDuckGo, and to a SearXNG server if you set one up. For news questions they also go to GDELT (api.gdeltproject.org), and Sidekick downloads Wikipedia's current-events pages and any news feeds you added (these downloads send no question words). Nothing else is sent: no memory, photos, voice, location (except for weather, below), contacts or identifiers. Those services see your IP address and the search words, under their own privacy policies. If there's no internet, a question or research you asked for waits on the phone (a few days at most) and is looked up once you're back online; its card in the chat has Cancel.\n3. **Google features — off unless you turn them on.** If you sign in with Google for a feature, your phone talks directly to Google; nothing goes through us:\n   - **Email:** sends an email you have approved, using Gmail (permission to send only; Sidekick cannot read your mail).\n   - **Google Tasks:** keeps your Sidekick to-dos and Google Tasks in step.\n   - **Google Drive backup:** uploads your exported backup file to your Drive. Sidekick can only see files it created.\n\n   Google's privacy policy applies to that data. You can turn each feature off in Settings, and remove Sidekick's access at myaccount.google.com/permissions. Sidekick's use of information received from Google APIs follows the [Google API Services User Data Policy](https://developers.google.com/terms/api-services-user-data-policy), including the Limited Use requirements.\n\n4. **Weather — only with online lookup on, when you ask about the weather.** Your location, rounded to about 1 km, is sent to the Norwegian Meteorological Institute (api.met.no) to get the forecast. Nothing else is sent. Also with online lookup on, when you ask where you are, your position is sent to Google's address lookup built into Android to get the street name.\n5. **Links and apps you open.** When you tap a link, or send a message through another app (such as Messages or WhatsApp), that app or website handles it under its own policy. Directions you ask for while offline wait on the phone (3 hours at most) and are handed to your maps app once you're back online, when you tap them or right away if you asked a few minutes before. **Report** under a picture Sidekick drew opens your email app with a draft to us (the reason and what was asked for, no picture); nothing is sent unless you send it.\n\n6. **Quick fixes — on unless you turn them off** (Settings → Advanced → Get quick fixes). On Wi-Fi, at most every few hours, Sidekick asks Expo (expo.dev, the service that delivers small app fixes) whether a fix is available. The request contains only the app's version information (runtime version, platform, update channel and the current update's id); like any internet request, Expo sees your phone's IP address. No personal information, memories or usage data are sent. Turned off, Sidekick never contacts Expo and fixes arrive with Google Play updates.\n\nFull app updates come through Google Play.\n\n## Camera, microphone and other permissions\n\nSidekick asks for each permission when you first use the feature that needs it. You can refuse or withdraw it in the app's Settings or in Android settings. The camera and microphone are only used while you have listening or the camera turned on, and Android shows its indicator whenever they are in use. Consent mode, which is on by default where the law requires everyone's agreement to record, keeps nothing of voices that haven't agreed.\n\nIf you turn the live camera on, it keeps filming while your phone is locked, until you pause or stop it from the notification. You can turn this off in Settings → Camera & audio. Video and frames stay on the phone.\n\nPhotos permission (iPhone only): asked only when you turn on photo search. You can allow all photos or only the ones you choose, and change it at any time in Settings → Phone search or the phone's settings. On Android, Sidekick does not ask for access to your photos: you pick the photo folders it may read with the phone's own folder picker. The place a photo was taken, when the photo has one, is named using the list of towns built into the app. Folders and files need no permission: you pick each one with the phone's own picker, and Sidekick sees only what you picked (it never asks for access to all files).\n\nIf you turn on **Understand other languages around me** (off by default), lines heard in other languages are translated on the phone; nothing is sent over the network.\n\n## Children\n\nSidekick is not directed at children under 13 and does not knowingly collect information from anyone. Since nothing leaves the phone, nothing is collected from children either.\n\n## Changes\n\nIf this policy changes, the new version will be posted at the same address, with a new date at the top, and mentioned in the app's \"What's new\".\n\n## Contact\n\nQuestions? Email **info@sashy.ai**, or use **Settings → About → Send feedback** in the app.\n\nThis policy is published at https://robert-whiteley.github.io/sidekick-site/privacy.\n";

